## Final Judgement
## Step 1: Key Insights Summary

The fact-check confirms both parts of the claim. First, regarding population: United Nations projections estimate Nigeria's population in 2020 at approximately 208–213 million, well above the 200 million threshold stated in the claim. Second, regarding "multiple challenges": authoritative sources including the World Bank, BBC, and Nigerian government reports document severe economic challenges (worst recession since the 1980s, oil price collapse, COVID-19 impacts, rising poverty), security challenges (Boko Haram insurgency killing at least 27,000 people, kidnappings, armed robbery), and social challenges (corruption, unemployment exceeding 20%, nearly half the population in extreme poverty). Additionally, the exact quote has been verified in the official transcript of President Buhari's October 1, 2020 Independence Day speech.

## Step 2: Missing Information

The evidence is comprehensive and clear on all aspects of the claim. No further information is needed.

## Step 3: Conclusion

The claim that Nigeria's population exceeds 200 million is supported by UN demographic data, and the claim that Nigeria faces "multiple challenges" is corroborated by multiple reputable international and domestic sources documenting severe economic, security, and social crises around October 2020. Therefore, the fact-check clearly supports the claim. The decision is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The claim is supported by evidence that Nigeria's population in 2020 was approximately 208–213 million according to [UN projections](https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/population-growth-rate), and that the country faced multiple severe challenges around October 2020. These include a historic economic recession driven by oil price collapse and the COVID-19 pandemic, as reported by the [World Bank](https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2020/06/25/nigerias-economy-faces-worst-recession-in-four-decades-says-new-world-bank-report), along with security threats such as the Boko Haram insurgency documented by [VOA News](https://www.voanews.com/a/africa_nigerian-idps-citizens-hope-better-202/6181940.html), and broader social challenges like poverty and unemployment noted by the [BBC](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-47381382). The exact statement was also verified in the [official transcript](https://www.channelstv.com/2020/10/01/nigeria-at-60-president-buharis-independence-anniversary-speech-full-text/) of President Buhari's October 1, 2020 speech.